{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a rational choice based on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) rather than probability, accepting the cost that verisimilitude remains a regulative ideal without a formal calculus.","counterconditions":["If the Tichý/Miller proof that verisimilitude is undefined in deterministic frameworks holds universally, this defense collapses.","If 'acting on' a theory logically entails assigning it higher subjective probability, the distinction fails."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between logical probability (which decreases with content) and corroboration (which reports past performance). The preference for acting on the best-tested theory is a methodological rule to select the theory with the highest potential verisimilitude, acknowledging it might still be false. This avoids induction because it does not claim the theory is *likely* true, only that it is the *best available guess* given severe testing.","optional_refs":["4c1f39b88c2aff008393b049ab69e2d3916b241903dc9cf1cba92d89c4bd3a0a","7f3ec7042a0d78a88d129dff6936bdac5c25240b52bb6a22bd17baf0bfb2443b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles induction back in because any decision to 'act on' a theory necessarily assumes the future will resemble the past (uniformity of nature), which is the very principle Popper sought to eliminate.","counterconditions":["If one can act purely on the basis of eliminating error without any expectation of future success.","If 'preference' is redefined as a non-cognitive volitional act devoid of expectation."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"To prefer Theory A over Theory B for action implies an expectation that A will perform better in the future. This expectation is an inductive inference from past test results to future performance. Without this inductive leap, past corroboration is historically interesting but practically irrelevant for future action.","optional_refs":["66225d91beb60a53b7f8f9c10281638da03df4b726808e77b4bfb8db6cd0919e","7029b8beeca6862492b2d4be4252d24fc1970bf8031490c031541381fa2b714a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible as a 'critical rationalist' wager: we choose the best-tested theory not because it is likely true, but because it has survived attempts to falsify it, making it the most informative error-eliminated option available.","counterconditions":["If the 'survivor' status provides no pragmatic advantage over a random guess in a chaotic universe.","If the definition of 'severe test' secretly relies on inductive assumptions about test validity."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"This approach treats science as an evolutionary process of conjecture and refutation. Preference is not a judgment of truth probability but a strategic move in a game of error elimination. We act on the survivor because it is the only one not yet shown to be wrong in specific domains, maximizing our exposure to potential new errors (learning).","optional_refs":["6d2476f30f531f1d40759d5aa09b806c4d842b74521e7a52a007df516beed4f2","7bd4408ca50f95ce68936c9cedda119212c17568726c6331d7e74fe0b8ffc86b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma dissolves if we view corroboration as a report of past performance only, and 'preference' as a non-rational, psychological commitment required for action, separating the logic of discovery from the psychology of decision.","counterconditions":["If Popper claimed his system provided a complete guide to rational action, not just logical assessment.","If the 'psychological commitment' cannot be distinguished from implicit probabilistic belief."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper strictly separates the logical status of a theory (unproven) from the psychological state of the scientist. The 'preference' is a psychological necessity for acting in an uncertain world, not a logical derivation. It smuggles nothing in because it makes no logical claim about the future; it is a voluntary leap of faith consistent with fallibilism.","optional_refs":["a9f9ff400459ab6e301346b3d488c3b28dbc247dda9fc511e0bb8b1504a0ba48","5d43a9c25315be3dc0114665380b1138afc924713ce907890005aaaa1687ac23"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible via a 'propensity' interpretation of probability, where corroboration measures the objective propensity of a theory to survive tests, distinct from subjective Bayesian probability.","counterconditions":["If propensity interpretations fail to solve the problem of induction regarding single-case events.","If Popper explicitly rejected applying propensity to theory choice in this manner."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper later explored propensity theory. Corroboration could be seen as evidence of a theory's objective propensity to withstand falsification attempts. This allows a form of 'probability' (propensity) that is not inductive in the Humean sense (subjective expectation) but ontological (a property of the situation/theory).","optional_refs":["354fa70c0e0698475f3990a6611ddf1b45577f6071bdf6fcf8f9490f23ab5688","e28e1e645a8e8645a0d0cfba32f0ad1e7f00d2fb5dd8fb2cf94cc4d1679958be"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}